Alice casts Kindle targeting Orim. Since no Kindle cards are in graveyards, X = 2, so Orim takes 2 damage. Bob taps Orim to prevent the next 3 damage, reducing the 2 damage to 0. Alice then casts her second Kindle targeting Orim. Again X = 2 (no Kindle in graveyards yet), so Orim would take 2 damage, but Orim's prevention ability has already been used this turn and only prevents the next 3 damage once — actually, the ability says "the next 3 damage that would be dealt to any target this turn," so after the first use, the prevention is consumed. Wait — actually, the ability triggers each time it's activated; Bob can tap Orim again? No — Orim is untapped after the first tap, so it can't be tapped again this turn. So the second Kindle deals 2 damage to Orim, reducing its toughness from 3 to 1. Orim survives with 1 damage marked.

Bob cannot prevent the second Kindle because Orim is already tapped. Alice does not kill Orim outright but reduces it to 1 toughness. Bob has no further prevention available this turn.

Optimal play: Alice casts both Kindles, dealing 2 damage each (total 2 after prevention), leaving Orim at 1/3 with 2 damage marked. Bob taps Orim once to prevent the first Kindle's damage. No further prevention is possible. Orim survives the turn. [end of text]


